  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two experimental strategies for discovering drug-resistance mechanisms in cultured cells. On the left, a “target focused” approach starts with a mammalian expression plasmid carrying a specific target open reading frame. This vector is first propagated in error-prone E. coli so the target gene accumulates mutations, then introduced into a drug-sensitive cell line. After drug treatment, the surviving pooled cells are analyzed by sequencing the plasmid DNA, allowing researchers to identify mutations in the chosen target that confer resistance.\n\nOn the right, a “target agnostic” strategy does not begin with a predefined gene. Instead, a drug-sensitive mismatch repair-deficient cell line, which is prone to generating mutations across the genome, is exposed to the drug. Surviving clones are then isolated and their genomes are sequenced, apparently by whole-exome or transcriptome analysis, to reveal resistance-associated changes anywhere in the cell.\n\nOverall, the visual emphasizes the contrast between a hypothesis-driven screen restricted to one candidate target and an unbiased genome-wide selection approach that can uncover broader resistance pathways.",
